.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt PROVIDER-ENCODER 7ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm provider-encoder
.Nd The OSSL_ENCODER library \(la-\(ra provider functions
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In openssl/core_dispatch.h
.Ft /*
  * None of these are actual functions, but are displayed like this for
  * the function signatures for functions that are offered as function
  * pointers in OSSL_DISPATCH arrays.
  */

 /* Encoder parameter accessor and descriptor */
 const OSSL_PARAM *
.Fo OSSL_FUNC_encoder_gettable_params
.Fa "void *provctx"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo OSSL_FUNC_encoder_get_params
.Fa "OSSL_PARAM params[]"
.Fc
.Ft /* Functions to construct / destruct / manipulate the encoder context */
 void *
.Fo OSSL_FUNC_encoder_newctx
.Fa "void *provctx"
.Fc
.Ft void
.Fo OSSL_FUNC_encoder_freectx
.Fa "void *ctx"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo OSSL_FUNC_encoder_set_ctx_params
.Fa "void *ctx"
.Fa "const OSSL_PARAM params[]"
.Fc
.Ft const OSSL_PARAM *
.Fo OSSL_FUNC_encoder_settable_ctx_params
.Fa "void *provctx"
.Fc
.Ft /* Functions to check selection support */
 int
.Fo OSSL_FUNC_encoder_does_selection
.Fa "void *provctx"
.Fa "int selection"
.Fc
.Ft /* Functions to encode object data */
 int
.Fo OSSL_FUNC_encoder_encode
.Fa "void *ctx"
.Fa "OSSL_CORE_BIO *out"
.Fa "const void *obj_raw"
.Fa "const OSSL_PARAM obj_abstract[]"
.Fa "int selection"
.Fa "OSSL_PASSPHRASE_CALLBACK *cb"
.Fa "void *cbarg"
.Fc
.Ft /* Functions to import and free a temporary object to be encoded */
 void *
.Fo OSSL_FUNC_encoder_import_object
.Fa "void *ctx"
.Fa "int selection"
.Fa "const OSSL_PARAM params[]"
.Fc
.Ft void
.Fo OSSL_FUNC_encoder_free_object
.Fa "void *obj"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Em \&We use the wide term \(dqencode" in this manual. This includes but is not limited to serialization.
.Pp
The ENCODER operation is a generic method to encode a provider-native
object
.Pf ( Fa obj_raw )
or an object abstraction
.Pf ( Em object_abstract ,
see
.Xr provider-object 7 )
into an encoded form, and write the result to the given OSSL_CORE_BIO.
If the caller wants to get the encoded stream to memory, it should
provide a
.Xr BIO_s_mem 3
.Sy BIO .
.Pp
The encoder doesn't need to know more about the
.Vt OSSL_CORE_BIO
pointer than being able to pass it to the appropriate BIO upcalls (see
.Xr provider-base 7 Ns ).
.Pp
The ENCODER implementation may be part of a chain, where data is passed
from one to the next.
For example, there may be an implementation to encode an object to DER
(that object is assumed to be provider-native and thereby passed via
.Fa obj_raw Ns ),
and another one that encodes DER to PEM (that one would receive the DER
encoding via
.Em obj_abstract Ns ).
.Pp
The encoding using the
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
array form allows a encoder to be used for data that's been exported
from another provider, and thereby allow them to exist independently of
each other.
.Pp
The encoding using a provider side object can only be safely used with
provider data coming from the same provider, for example keys with the
.Xr provider-keymgmt 7
provider.
.Pp
All "functions" mentioned here are passed as function pointers between
.Pa libcrypto
and the provider in
.Xr OSSL_DISPATCH 3
arrays via
.Xr OSSL_ALGORITHM 3
arrays that are returned by the provider's
.Xr provider_query_operation 3
function (see
.Xr provider-base 7 Ns ).
.Pp
All these "functions" have a corresponding function type definition
named
.Sy OSSL_FUNC_{name}_fn ,
and a helper function to retrieve the function pointer from an
.Xr OSSL_DISPATCH 3
element named
.Sy OSSL_FUNC_{name} .
For example, the "function"
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_encoder_encode
has these:
.Bd -literal
 typedef int
     (OSSL_FUNC_encoder_encode_fn)(void *ctx, OSSL_CORE_BIO *out,
                                   const void *obj_raw,
                                   const OSSL_PARAM obj_abstract[],
                                   int selection,
                                   OSSL_PASSPHRASE_CALLBACK *cb, void *cbarg);
 static ossl_inline OSSL_FUNC_encoder_encode_fn
     OSSL_FUNC_encoder_encode(const OSSL_DISPATCH *opf);
.Ed
.Pp
.Xr OSSL_DISPATCH 3
arrays are indexed by numbers that are provided as macros in
.Xr openssl-core_dispatch.h 7 ,
as follows:
.Bd -literal
 OSSL_FUNC_encoder_get_params          OSSL_FUNC_ENCODER_GET_PARAMS
 OSSL_FUNC_encoder_gettable_params     OSSL_FUNC_ENCODER_GETTABLE_PARAMS

 OSSL_FUNC_encoder_newctx              OSSL_FUNC_ENCODER_NEWCTX
 OSSL_FUNC_encoder_freectx             OSSL_FUNC_ENCODER_FREECTX
 OSSL_FUNC_encoder_set_ctx_params      OSSL_FUNC_ENCODER_SET_CTX_PARAMS
 OSSL_FUNC_encoder_settable_ctx_params OSSL_FUNC_ENCODER_SETTABLE_CTX_PARAMS

 OSSL_FUNC_encoder_does_selection      OSSL_FUNC_ENCODER_DOES_SELECTION

 OSSL_FUNC_encoder_encode              OSSL_FUNC_ENCODER_ENCODE

 OSSL_FUNC_encoder_import_object       OSSL_FUNC_ENCODER_IMPORT_OBJECT
 OSSL_FUNC_encoder_free_object         OSSL_FUNC_ENCODER_FREE_OBJECT
.Ed
.Ss Names and properties
The name of an implementation should match the type of object it
handles.
For example, an implementation that encodes an RSA key should be named
"RSA". Likewise, an implementation that further encodes DER should be
named "DER".
.Pp
Properties, as defined in the
.Xr OSSL_ALGORITHM 3
array element of each decoder implementation, can be used to further
specify details about an implementation:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It output
This property is used to specify what type of output the implementation
produces.
.Pp
This property is
.Em mandatory .
.Pp
OpenSSL providers recognize the following output types:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It text
An implementation with that output type outputs human readable text,
making that implementation suitable for
.Qo Li -text Qc
output in diverse
.Xr openssl 1
commands.
.It pem
An implementation with that output type outputs PEM formatted data.
.It der
An implementation with that output type outputs DER formatted data.
.It msblob
An implementation with that output type outputs MSBLOB formatted data.
.It pvk
An implementation with that output type outputs PVK formatted data.
.El
.It structure
This property is used to specify the structure that is used for the
encoded object.
An example could be
.Qo Li pkcs8 Qc ,
to specify explicitly that an object (presumably an asymmetric key pair,
in this case) will be wrapped in a PKCS#8 structure as part of the
encoding.
.Pp
This property is
.Em optional .
.El
.Pp
The possible values of both these properties is open ended.
A provider may very well specify output types and structures that
libcrypto doesn't know anything about.
.Ss Subset selections
Sometimes, an object has more than one subset of data that is
interesting to treat separately or together.
It's possible to specify what subsets are to be encoded, with a set of
bits
.Fa selection
that are passed in an
.Vt int .
.Pp
This set of bits depend entirely on what kind of provider-side object is
passed.
For example, those bits are assumed to be the same as those used with
.Xr provider-keymgmt 7
(see
.Xr provider-keymgmt 7 )
when the object is an asymmetric keypair.
.Pp
ENCODER implementations are free to regard the
.Fa selection
as a set of hints, but must do so with care.
In the end, the output must make sense, and if there's a corresponding
decoder, the resulting decoded object must match the original object
that was encoded.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_encoder_does_selection
should tell if a particular implementation supports any of the
combinations given by
.Fa selection .
.Ss Context functions
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_encoder_newctx
returns a context to be used with the rest of the functions.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_encoder_freectx
frees the given
.Fa ctx ,
if it was created by
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_encoder_newctx .
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_encoder_set_ctx_params
sets context data according to parameters from
.Em params
that it recognises.
Unrecognised parameters should be ignored.
Passing NULL for
.Em params
should return true.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_encoder_settable_ctx_params
returns a constant
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
array describing the parameters that
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_encoder_set_ctx_params
can handle.
.Pp
See
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
for further details on the parameters structure used by
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_encoder_set_ctx_params
and
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_encoder_settable_ctx_params .
.Ss Import functions
A provider-native object may be associated with a foreign provider, and
may therefore be unsuitable for direct use with a given ENCODER
implementation.
Provided that the foreign provider's implementation to handle the object
has a function to export that object in
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
array form, the ENCODER implementation should be able to import that
array and create a suitable object to be passed to
OSSL_FUNC_encoder_encode()'s
.Fa obj_raw .
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_encoder_import_object
should import the subset of
.Em params
given with
.Fa selection
to create a provider-native object that can be passed as
.Fa obj_raw
to
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_encoder_encode .
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_encoder_free_object
should free the object that was created with
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_encoder_import_object .
.Ss Encoding functions
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_encoder_encode
should take a provider-native object (in
.Fa obj_raw )
or an object abstraction (in
.Em obj_abstract Ns ),
and should output the object in encoded form to the
.Vt OSSL_CORE_BIO .
The
.Fa selection
bits, if relevant, should determine in greater detail what will be
output.
The encoding functions also take an
.Xr OSSL_PASSPHRASE_CALLBACK 3
function pointer along with a pointer to application data
.Fa cbarg ,
which should be used when a pass phrase prompt is needed.
.Ss Encoder operation parameters
Operation parameters currently recognised by built-in encoders are as
follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \(dqcipher" ( Ns Sy OSSL_ENCODER_PARAM_CIPHER Ns ) <UTF8 string>
The name of the encryption cipher to be used when generating encrypted
encoding.
This is used when encoding private keys, as well as other objects that
need protection.
.Pp
If this name is invalid for the encoding implementation, the
implementation should refuse to perform the encoding, i.e.
.Xr OSSL_FUNC_encoder_encode_data 3
and
.Xr OSSL_FUNC_encoder_encode_object 3
should return an error.
.It \(dqproperties" ( Ns Sy OSSL_ENCODER_PARAM_PROPERTIES Ns ) <UTF8 string>
The properties to be queried when trying to fetch the algorithm given
with the "cipher" parameter.
This must be given together with the "cipher" parameter to be considered
valid.
.Pp
The encoding implementation isn't obligated to use this value.
However, it is recommended that implementations that do not handle
property strings return an error on receiving this parameter unless its
value NULL or the empty string.
.It \(dqsave-parameters" ( Ns Sy OSSL_ENCODER_PARAM_SAVE_PARAMETERS Ns ) <integer>
If set to 0 disables saving of key domain parameters.
Default is 1. It currently has an effect only on DSA keys.
.El
.Pp
Parameters currently recognised by the built-in pass phrase callback:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It \(dqinfo" ( Ns Sy OSSL_PASSPHRASE_PARAM_INFO Ns ) <UTF8 string>
A string of information that will become part of the pass phrase prompt.
This could be used to give the user information on what kind of object
it's being prompted for.
.El
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_encoder_newctx
returns a pointer to a context, or NULL on failure.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_encoder_set_ctx_params
returns 1, unless a recognised parameter was invalid or caused an error,
for which 0 is returned.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_encoder_settable_ctx_params
returns a pointer to an array of constant
.Xr OSSL_PARAM 3
elements.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_encoder_does_selection
returns 1 if the encoder implementation supports any of the
.Fa selection
bits, otherwise 0.
.Pp
.Fn OSSL_FUNC_encoder_encode
returns 1 on success, or 0 on failure.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr provider 7
.Sh HISTORY
The ENCODER interface was introduced in OpenSSL 3.0.
